{%- comment -%}
  One cart line.

  Quantity is described as "entries" throughout, because on this store a line
  quantity is literally the number of entries bought.

  Parameters:
    item     (required) a cart line item
    context  'drawer' or 'page'
    index    (required) the line's position, passed in from the caller's loop
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign entry_total = item.quantity
  assign max_per_person = item.product.metafields.custom.max_entries_per_person.value

  comment
    The size FILTER, not `.size`. Line item properties come through as a drop,
    and a drop answers an unknown property with nil rather than an error, so
    `item.properties.size` silently evaluated to nil and the whole block below
    was skipped on every cart that had properties. The filter reads the
    collection itself and returns a real count.
  endcomment
  assign property_count = item.properties | size

  comment
    `render` does not inherit the caller's forloop, so `forloop.index` was
    always empty here and every line in the basket was given the same id:
    CartQty-page-. Two competitions in one basket meant two inputs sharing an
    id, and two labels pointing at whichever the browser matched first, so
    clicking the second line's label moved focus to the first line's field.
    The position comes in from the caller instead.
  endcomment
  assign line_index = index | default: forloop.index
-%}
<li class="cart-item" data-cart-item data-line-key="{{ item.key }}" data-line-index="{{ line_index }}">
  <a class="cart-item__media" href="{{ item.url }}" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
    {%- if item.image -%}
      <img
        src="{{ item.image | image_url: width: 160 }}"
        srcset="{{ item.image | image_url: width: 160 }} 160w, {{ item.image | image_url: width: 240 }} 240w"
        sizes="80px"
        alt=""
        width="80"
        height="80"
        loading="lazy"
      >
    {%- else -%}
      {%- render 'placeholder', name: 'product-1' -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </a>

  <div class="cart-item__details">
    <a class="cart-item__title" href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.product.title | escape }}</a>

    {%- unless item.product.has_only_default_variant -%}
      <p class="cart-item__variant">{{ item.variant.title | escape }}</p>
    {%- endunless -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The skill answer shows here so the entrant can check it before paying —
      the last point at which a misclick can be corrected. Properties whose key
      starts with an underscore (_skill_question, _entry_route) are Shopify's
      convention for bookkeeping the customer should not see, so they are
      carried on the order but never rendered.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if property_count > 0 -%}
      <dl class="cart-item__properties">
        {%- for property in item.properties -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            `slice: 0` for the same reason: a key is a string, and a string has
            no `first`, so `property.first.first` was nil and never matched the
            underscore it was meant to catch.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- assign property_key_start = property.first | slice: 0 -%}
          {%- if property.last != blank and property_key_start != '_' -%}
            <div>
              <dt>{{ property.first | escape }}:</dt>
              <dd>{{ property.last | escape }}</dd>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </dl>
    {%- endif -%}

    <p class="cart-item__unit text-xs text-muted">
      {{ item.original_price | money }} {{ 'products.competition.per_entry' | t }}
    </p>

    {%- if item.line_level_discount_allocations.size > 0 -%}
      <ul class="cart-item__discounts">
        {%- for allocation in item.line_level_discount_allocations -%}
          <li>{{ allocation.discount_application.title }} (&minus;{{ allocation.amount | money }})</li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="cart-item__controls">
      <gs-quantity class="quantity-selector quantity-selector--small">
        <button
          type="button"
          class="quantity-selector__button"
          data-quantity-decrease
          aria-label="{{ 'products.competition.decrease' | t }}"
        >
          {%- render 'icon', icon: 'minus', size: 14 -%}
        </button>
        <label class="visually-hidden" for="CartQty-{{ context }}-{{ line_index }}">
          {{ 'products.competition.quantity_label' | t }}
        </label>
        <input
          id="CartQty-{{ context }}-{{ line_index }}"
          class="quantity-selector__input"
          type="number"
          inputmode="numeric"
          name="updates[]"
          value="{{ item.quantity }}"
          min="0"
          {% if max_per_person != blank and max_per_person > 0 %}max="{{ max_per_person }}"{% endif %}
          step="1"
          data-cart-quantity
          data-line-key="{{ item.key }}"
        >
        <button
          type="button"
          class="quantity-selector__button"
          data-quantity-increase
          aria-label="{{ 'products.competition.increase' | t }}"
        >
          {%- render 'icon', icon: 'plus', size: 14 -%}
        </button>
      </gs-quantity>

      <button
        type="button"
        class="cart-item__remove"
        data-cart-remove
        data-line-key="{{ item.key }}"
        aria-label="{{ 'cart.remove_item' | t: title: item.product.title }}"
      >
        {%- render 'icon', icon: 'trash', size: 16 -%}
        <span class="hide-mobile">{{ 'cart.remove' | t }}</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      The per-person cap is not stated here either. See
      snippets/entry-selector.liquid for why it cannot be enforced.
    {%- endcomment -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      A basket holding two competitions holds two different deadlines, and
      until now neither was shown. Someone deciding whether to check out now or
      later has no way to tell which one is about to close.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p class="cart-item__closing text-xs text-muted">
      {%- render 'competition-closing', product: item.product, icon: false -%}
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="cart-item__price">
    <span class="cart-item__line-total" data-line-total>{{ item.final_line_price | money }}</span>
    <span class="cart-item__line-count text-xs text-muted">
      {{- 'cart.entry_count' | t: count: entry_total -}}
    </span>
  </div>

  <p class="cart-item__error text-xs" data-line-error role="alert" hidden></p>
</li>
